1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,780
Start here.

2
00:00:01,930 --> 00:00:04,520
If you just realized you've been scammed,

3
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:05,560
take a breath.

4
00:00:05,830 --> 00:00:08,700
You are not the first person this has happened to,

5
00:00:08,650 --> 00:00:11,680
and there are clear steps you can take right now.

6
00:00:11,830 --> 00:00:14,660
What you do next depends on one thing:

7
00:00:14,810 --> 00:00:16,360
what you gave them.

8
00:00:16,630 --> 00:00:17,260
Money.

9
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:18,680
Personal information.

10
00:00:18,870 --> 00:00:21,400
Or access to your phone or computer.

11
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:23,860
This video walks through each one,

12
00:00:23,810 --> 00:00:26,360
using the steps the Federal Trade Commission publishes.

13
00:00:26,310 --> 00:00:29,600
Find the part that matches your situation,

14
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:32,400
and if it's more than one, do them all.

15
00:00:32,770 --> 00:00:34,520
A quick note before we start.

16
00:00:34,730 --> 00:00:37,600
I can't promise anyone gets their money back.

17
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:41,420
The FTC itself says your money might already be gone.

18
00:00:41,590 --> 00:00:45,560
But asking is free, and it is always worth asking.

19
00:00:46,059 --> 00:00:47,349
If you paid.

20
00:00:47,879 --> 00:00:50,709
The right call depends on how you paid,

21
00:00:50,659 --> 00:00:52,789
so let's go one by one.

22
00:00:53,039 --> 00:00:56,289
If you paid by credit card, contact the company that issued

23
00:00:56,239 --> 00:00:57,909
the card right away.

24
00:00:57,999 --> 00:00:59,809
Use the number on the back of the card,

25
00:00:59,759 --> 00:01:01,749
or log in to your account.

26
00:01:01,919 --> 00:01:05,549
Tell them it was a fraudulent charge and ask for a refund.

27
00:01:05,739 --> 00:01:09,829
The FTC notes that federal law protects you from unauthorized use

28
00:01:09,779 --> 00:01:11,209
of your credit card.

29
00:01:11,799 --> 00:01:13,489
Debit card works the same way.

30
00:01:13,439 --> 00:01:16,029
Contact your bank or credit union,

31
00:01:15,979 --> 00:01:17,989
use the number on the back of the card,

32
00:01:17,939 --> 00:01:20,269
report it, and ask for a refund.

33
00:01:20,459 --> 00:01:24,429
The FTC says federal protections apply to unauthorized debit card

34
00:01:24,379 --> 00:01:25,489
use too.

35
00:01:25,839 --> 00:01:28,769
If you paid with a gift card, contact the company that

36
00:01:28,719 --> 00:01:31,529
issued the card, using the number on the back.

37
00:01:31,559 --> 00:01:34,309
Tell them a scammer tricked you into giving up the card

38
00:01:34,259 --> 00:01:36,949
number and PIN, and ask for a refund.

39
00:01:37,079 --> 00:01:39,369
Keep the card and keep the receipt.

40
00:01:39,459 --> 00:01:42,849
If you sent a wire transfer, report it to the wire

41
00:01:42,799 --> 00:01:45,809
transfer company or to your bank right away.

42
00:01:45,859 --> 00:01:48,349
Say a scammer tricked you into sending money,

43
00:01:48,299 --> 00:01:50,349
and ask them to reverse it.

44
00:01:50,519 --> 00:01:53,249
If you used a payment app, report it to the app

45
00:01:53,199 --> 00:01:56,249
and ask it to reverse the payment and refund you.

46
00:01:56,199 --> 00:01:59,089
If that app is linked to a credit or debit card,

47
00:01:59,039 --> 00:02:01,769
report it to the card company or bank as well.

48
00:02:01,919 --> 00:02:04,709
If you made a bank transfer, report it to your bank

49
00:02:04,659 --> 00:02:07,089
or credit union and ask them to reverse it.

50
00:02:07,039 --> 00:02:11,809
If you paid with cryptocurrency, contact the exchange or the crypto

51
00:02:11,759 --> 00:02:15,449
ATM company you used, tell them the transaction was fraudulent,

52
00:02:15,399 --> 00:02:17,609
and ask them to reverse it.

53
00:02:17,659 --> 00:02:19,409
I'll be honest about this one:

54
00:02:19,379 --> 00:02:23,369
the FTC says crypto doesn't have the same legal protections as

55
00:02:23,319 --> 00:02:26,649
cards, and payments are typically not reversible.

56
00:02:26,759 --> 00:02:29,489
You can usually only get the money back if the person

57
00:02:29,439 --> 00:02:31,669
who received it sends it back.

58
00:02:31,759 --> 00:02:35,169
Still report it, and report it to the FTC,

59
00:02:35,219 --> 00:02:36,649
which I'll cover in a moment.

60
00:02:36,759 --> 00:02:39,929
And if you mailed cash, contact the U.S.

61
00:02:39,879 --> 00:02:42,229
Postal Inspection Service right away.

62
00:02:42,279 --> 00:02:48,089
The number is 1-877-876-2455.

63
00:02:48,039 --> 00:02:50,909
The FTC says that for a fee, they may be able

64
00:02:50,859 --> 00:02:54,589
to intercept a domestic package that hasn't been delivered yet.

65
00:02:54,559 --> 00:02:56,769
If you used another delivery company,

66
00:02:56,799 --> 00:02:58,929
contact them as soon as possible.

67
00:02:59,413 --> 00:03:01,843
If you shared personal information.

68
00:03:02,053 --> 00:03:04,703
If you gave someone your Social Security number,

69
00:03:04,653 --> 00:03:07,343
go to IdentityTheft.gov.

70
00:03:07,293 --> 00:03:10,143
If a scammer used the number, you can report it there

71
00:03:10,093 --> 00:03:12,683
and get a personalized recovery plan.

72
00:03:12,753 --> 00:03:15,343
If it wasn't used, or you're not sure,

73
00:03:15,433 --> 00:03:19,423
the FTC points you to the data breach steps on the same site.

74
00:03:19,593 --> 00:03:22,123
If you gave away a username and password,

75
00:03:22,073 --> 00:03:25,543
and you can still log in, change the password now and

76
00:03:25,493 --> 00:03:27,463
make it strong and unique.

77
00:03:27,593 --> 00:03:30,503
If you used the same password anywhere else,

78
00:03:30,473 --> 00:03:31,863
change it there too.

79
00:03:31,993 --> 00:03:34,603
Then turn on two-factor authentication.

80
00:03:34,973 --> 00:03:38,543
If you can't log in anymore, use the account company's recovery

81
00:03:38,493 --> 00:03:42,043
steps, and the FTC has a guide for getting back into

82
00:03:41,993 --> 00:03:43,223
a hacked account.

83
00:03:43,742 --> 00:03:46,152
If they got into your phone or computer.

84
00:03:46,342 --> 00:03:48,352
If you let someone get remote access,

85
00:03:48,302 --> 00:03:51,932
or you clicked something that may have installed software you didn't

86
00:03:51,882 --> 00:03:55,512
want, the FTC suggests three steps.

87
00:03:55,962 --> 00:03:59,492
Update your security software, or if you don't have any,

88
00:03:59,502 --> 00:04:02,552
download one recommended by independent review sites,

89
00:04:02,502 --> 00:04:03,972
friends or family.

90
00:04:04,182 --> 00:04:07,112
Run a scan and delete anything it flags.

91
00:04:07,342 --> 00:04:11,632
Then change your passwords and turn on two-factor authentication.

92
00:04:11,842 --> 00:04:14,132
If you need help removing malware,

93
00:04:14,162 --> 00:04:16,392
ask the device maker's tech support,

94
00:04:16,342 --> 00:04:20,412
a company you trust, or a knowledgeable friend or family member.

95
00:04:20,940 --> 00:04:22,130
Report it.

96
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:27,690
Whatever happened, report it at ReportFraud.ftc.gov.

97
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:30,650
The FTC says reports help it build cases,

98
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:33,270
spot trends and educate the public.

99
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:37,470
Reporting can feel pointless, but those reports are how patterns

100
00:04:37,420 --> 00:04:38,510
get noticed.

101
00:04:39,094 --> 00:04:41,504
Warning signs worth remembering.

102
00:04:41,914 --> 00:04:44,984
Two things from the FTC are worth saving.

103
00:04:45,554 --> 00:04:46,004
First:

104
00:04:46,274 --> 00:04:49,344
the FTC says it will never threaten you,

105
00:04:49,294 --> 00:04:52,964
never tell you to transfer money to "protect it," and never

106
00:04:52,914 --> 00:04:55,924
tell you to withdraw cash or buy gold.

107
00:04:56,194 --> 00:04:59,544
If someone claiming to be from a government agency says any

108
00:04:59,494 --> 00:05:01,824
of that, it is a scam.

109
00:05:02,754 --> 00:05:03,204
Second:

110
00:05:03,474 --> 00:05:06,744
scammers often demand payment in one specific way,

111
00:05:06,694 --> 00:05:09,944
such as a bank transfer, wire transfer,

112
00:05:09,954 --> 00:05:13,404
cryptocurrency, a payment app or a gift card,

113
00:05:13,354 --> 00:05:16,044
and insist nothing else will work.

114
00:05:16,174 --> 00:05:19,944
The FTC treats that as a strong sign of a scam.

115
00:05:20,445 --> 00:05:21,995
Send this to someone.

116
00:05:22,325 --> 00:05:24,935
Being scammed does not mean you were foolish.

117
00:05:25,165 --> 00:05:28,435
These schemes are built by people who do this for a living.

118
00:05:28,685 --> 00:05:31,115
If you know someone who might be embarrassed to ask for

119
00:05:31,065 --> 00:05:33,075
help, send them this video.

120
00:05:33,385 --> 00:05:37,095
This channel gives general information, not legal or financial

121
00:05:37,045 --> 00:05:40,055
advice, and the official links are in the description.

122
00:05:40,125 --> 00:05:43,115
Next time, we'll look at a scam that's showing up in

123
00:05:43,065 --> 00:05:44,875
a lot of text messages.

